{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1144639-wyld-west-lumberjacks-and-being-a-garou","id":1144639,"title":"Wyld West Lumberjacks and Being a Garou?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Werewolf: The Apocalypse"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-09-27T22:40:59-07:00","id":1144639,"title":"Wyld West Lumberjacks and Being a Garou?","content":"So a player is joining that wants to be a Lumberjack Uktena Metis... what are the thoughts of resolving early Michigan Lumberjacks with Gaian, particularly pure tribe, principles? Basically he is going to be a Metis, bastard of Uktena and Wendigo, who his family had mostly raised by Wolf kin cause they disliked him. He had is first change and wandered off and found a lumberjack company, with a few kinfolk (maybe even european kinfolk) and spent over a decade, maybe even two as a lost cub lumberjack.<br /> <br /> There are a lot of abnormal bits tied into this, metis raised by wolves, native american living with americans, not having a rite of passage while you are still a teen, discovering a Sawshaped Grand Klaive. Lumberjacks who are also werewolves.<br /> <br /> I do remember talking about lumberjacks in early school since I had elementary school mostly in Michigan, but my instincts are conservation wasn't a thing yet? Any ideas or expertise on this subject? Could I say some lumberjack companies could have responsibly logged, while maybe I don't know secretly warring against eviler lumberfolk?<br /> <br /> Help please?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/754-eldagusto","name":"Eldagusto","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar754_2.gif","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-28T15:41:46-07:00","id":1144990,"title":"","content":"I think the very strong environmental message in Werewolf has less and less relevancy the further back in time you go. Centuries before the Europeans ever arrived in the Americas, the Indians had built great cities in Mexico and Peru, built the great mounds at Cahokia, created terra preta farms in Amazonia, and changed the woodlands of the northeastern United States into a mosaic of orchards and farms. So overall there were plenty of Indians who cut down trees. Then in the first centuries of European contact in Michigan, you had British and French lumbering to build the trading spots and forts for the fur trade.<br /> <br /> What would be changing in the 19th century was the scale of lumbering being done. By the 1840s, the northeast forests were being timbered out, and Michigan and the rest of the northern Midwest became the new center of the industry.<br /> <br /> But I do think there is room for a small scale lumbering enterprise done by kinfolk which even Garou might contribute to. However, the backdrop would also include the much larger great commercial logging camps that are beginning to disrupt the environment and Gaian spirits by their sheer scale.<br /> <br /> I wouldn't say the kinfolk logging camp is engaged in &quot;responsible logging&quot; which would be an unknown concept in the 19th century. But I would say the combination of 1) being much smaller in scale, and 2) the camp performing some kind of rites that placates the spirits of the area they are logging create a situation that Garou would find acceptable or tolerable. It would not be all that different than what had gone on before.<br /> <br /> Many of the modern themes of Werewolf are only becoming apparent during the 19th century. I think a productive approach is to have the PCs and NPCs find this situation (large scale commercial logging) as novel and begin the first conversations of, &quot;Is this something we should be concerned about / what do we need to be doing / what are our goals / what are our methods&quot; because before this time, this problem really has not existed before (but not entirely as one reason for the collapse of Cahokia and other towns in the Mississippian Culture was the erosion caused by too much clear cutting of trees). And of course, over time these larger logging operations will put pressure on this kinfolk operation because they are earning far more money than this smaller outfit.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-29T10:32:24-07:00","id":1145289,"title":"","content":"Perhaps a bit out of the time period, as I can not remember when the Wyld West ends, but a good book to give you a feel for early logging practices in the PNW is, <a href=\"http://www.worldcat.org/oclc/255582642\" target=\"_blank\"><i>The Golden Spruce</i></a>. A good book to show that the Wyrm is not behind every ecological assault by people.<br /> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kiidk%27yaas\" target=\"_blank\"><img src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/38/Kiidk%27yaas_in_1984.jpg\" class=\"bbcode-attachment bbcode-attachment--lightbox js-lightbox\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" itemprop=\"image\" loading=\"lazy\"/></a>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Lead Writer for the Savage Age of Werewolf: the Apocalypse: <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/428750/Ghost-Lineages-The-Advanced-Players-Guide-to-the-Savage-Age\" target=\"_blank\">Ghost Lineages</a>, <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/312700/The-Rise-of-the-Garou-The-Savage-Age-Volume-Two\" target=\"_blank\">Rise of the Garou</a>, <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/331302/Tools-of-Extinction-The-Savage-Age-Volume-Three\" target=\"_blank\">Tools of Extinction</a>, <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/352717/Tribebook-Wlewa\" target=\"_blank\">Tribebook: Wlewa</a>.<br /> <br /> Want RPGs in libraries? Check out my book, <a href=\"http://www.abc-clio.com/ABC-CLIOCorporate/product.aspx?pc=A3951P\" target=\"_blank\">Dragons in the Stacks</a>. Now with <a href=\"http://www.ennie-awards.com/blog/2015-ennie-award-nominees-and-spotlight-winners/\" target=\"_blank\">awards</a>!<br /> \u200b","edited":{"userid":1813,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1813-grimjaws","user":"grimjaws","date":". 09-29-2017, 01:35 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1813-grimjaws","name":"grimjaws","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1813_4.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"134"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}